# Frontend Conventions

## Stack
- **Jinja2** — server-side HTML templating
- **HTMX** — dynamic updates without JavaScript frameworks
- **Plain CSS** — no Tailwind, no Bootstrap, no CSS-in-JS

## No JavaScript Frameworks
Do not introduce React, Vue, Alpine.js, or any JS framework.
HTMX handles all dynamic behavior. If you find yourself writing significant
JavaScript, stop and reconsider — there is almost certainly an HTMX approach.

## Template Structure
```
app/templates/
  base.html          # Base layout: nav, CSS links, HTMX script tag
  index.html         # Main page: calendar + chat layout
  partials/          # Reusable fragments returned by HTMX requests
    calendar.html    # Calendar view fragment
    workout_card.html  # Single workout detail fragment
    chat_message.html  # Single chat message fragment
```

- All templates extend `base.html` via `{% extends "base.html" %}`
- Partials are standalone HTML fragments — no `<html>` or `<body>` tags
- Partials are returned by FastAPI routes in response to HTMX requests

## HTMX Patterns
- Use `hx-get` / `hx-post` to call FastAPI routes
- Use `hx-target` to specify which element to update
- Use `hx-swap` to control how content is replaced (default: `innerHTML`)
- Use `hx-trigger` to control when requests fire (default: user action)

```html
<!-- Good: clicking a workout fetches its detail and swaps into #workout-detail -->
<div class="workout-item"
     hx-get="/workouts/{{ workout.id }}"
     hx-target="#workout-detail"
     hx-swap="innerHTML">
  {{ workout.name }}
</div>

<!-- Good: chat form submits without page reload, appends response to #chat-messages -->
<form hx-post="/chat"
      hx-target="#chat-messages"
      hx-swap="beforeend">
  <input type="text" name="message" placeholder="Ask your coach...">
  <button type="submit">Send</button>
</form>
```

## UI Layout
The main page is divided into two panels:
- **Left panel (~2/3 width):** calendar view showing completed and upcoming workouts
- **Right panel (~1/3 width):** chat interface for agent interaction

```html
<!-- Rough layout structure -->
<div class="app-layout">
  <div class="calendar-panel">
    <!-- Calendar + workout details -->
  </div>
  <div class="chat-panel">
    <!-- Chat messages + input -->
  </div>
</div>
```

## CSS Conventions
- All styles live in `app/static/css/main.css`
- Use CSS custom properties (variables) for colors, spacing, and typography
- Mobile-friendly is nice but not the primary concern — this is a desktop app
- Keep styles simple and readable; avoid clever CSS tricks

```css
/* Define variables at the root for easy theming */
:root {
  --color-primary: #e05c1a;   /* cycling orange */
  --color-bg: #f9f9f9;
  --color-text: #1a1a1a;
  --color-border: #ddd;
  --spacing-sm: 8px;
  --spacing-md: 16px;
  --spacing-lg: 32px;
}
```

## Jinja2 Conventions
- Pass only what the template needs — don't pass entire model objects if a few fields suffice
- Use `{{ variable }}` for output, `{% %}` for logic
- Keep logic in templates minimal — if you need complex logic, do it in the route handler
- Use `{% for %}` loops and `{% if %}` blocks sparingly; heavy logic belongs in Python

## HTMX Script
Load HTMX from CDN in `base.html`. No need to install it via npm.

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/htmx.org@2.0.0" integrity="..." crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
```

Always pin to a specific HTMX version — do not use `@latest`.
